1
00:00:01,602 --> 00:00:03,161
♪♪

2
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:10,463
♪ Now I'm hoping tonight
you'll go, go to the right window ♪

3
00:02:10,597 --> 00:02:17,868
♪ Scatter your light, say
I'm all right, please do ♪

4
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:24,275
♪ Tell her that I'm
blue and lonely ♪

5
00:02:24,411 --> 00:02:31,716
♪ Dreamy Carolina moon ♪

6
00:02:31,852 --> 00:02:32,945
Ben?

7
00:02:33,086 --> 00:02:35,398
- Ben, will you please answer that door?
- Who could that be?

8
00:02:35,422 --> 00:02:37,323
- I don't know. It's almost 9:00.
- Ben!

9
00:02:37,457 --> 00:02:38,823
Ben! Ben!

10
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:40,689
I guess I'd better go answer it.

11
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:43,820
Ben, answer this door!

12
00:02:43,964 --> 00:02:48,664
- Ben!
- Oh, no, it's my cousin. It's Diana.

13
00:02:48,802 --> 00:02:50,327
Open the door!
Let me in the house!

14
00:02:50,470 --> 00:02:53,269
Thank goodness you're
home. We have to talk.

15
00:02:53,407 --> 00:02:56,775
All right. What would
you like to talk about?

16
00:02:56,910 --> 00:03:01,507
So, Diana, what brings you
over here at 9:00 at night?

17
00:03:01,648 --> 00:03:05,779
Ben, Ben, Jack left
me for another woman.

18
00:03:05,919 --> 00:03:08,218
Jack left me for another woman!

19
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:09,846
Diana, quit it!

20
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:14,122
Ben... Here. Sit down.
Come on, sit down.

21
00:03:14,261 --> 00:03:16,958
Now, when did this happen?

22
00:03:17,097 --> 00:03:18,998
Two weeks ago.

23
00:03:19,132 --> 00:03:21,226
I came home from the
hairdresser's, and he was gone.

24
00:03:21,368 --> 00:03:25,203
All his things were gone, too,
and I haven't heard from him since!

25
00:03:25,338 --> 00:03:27,068
There, there.

26
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:32,168
Ben, I'd love to stay
and chat with you folks,

27
00:03:32,312 --> 00:03:35,441
but it's getting on
toward my bedtime, so...

28
00:03:36,516 --> 00:03:38,382
But like I said,
I'd love to chat.

29
00:03:38,518 --> 00:03:40,783
Go! Just go.

30
00:03:42,689 --> 00:03:44,248
There, there, now.

31
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:48,287
What makes you think
it's another woman?

32
00:03:48,428 --> 00:03:52,126
Because he left a note saying
he was going to start over with Lisa

33
00:03:52,265 --> 00:03:54,598
and that his attorneys
would be in touch.

34
00:03:54,735 --> 00:03:56,169
Ohh...

35
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:58,170
Who's Lisa?

36
00:03:58,305 --> 00:04:02,800
Some would-be artist that he met
at this gallery he always patronized.

37
00:04:02,943 --> 00:04:03,943
I met her once.

38
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:06,036
Oh. Talk about space cadets.

39
00:04:06,179 --> 00:04:08,671
And young... She could
be doing Clearasil ads.

40
00:04:08,815 --> 00:04:10,750
Look, I know he's rich, but...

41
00:04:10,884 --> 00:04:13,353
I could understand an
affair with a Hollywood starlet

42
00:04:13,487 --> 00:04:15,422
or a professional tennis player,

43
00:04:15,555 --> 00:04:17,046
but some witless bimbo?

44
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:18,954
He must be out of his mind.

45
00:04:19,092 --> 00:04:20,754
Well, maybe it's just temporary.

46
00:04:20,894 --> 00:04:24,058
I mean, after all, you
were having problems.

47
00:04:24,197 --> 00:04:27,190
No, we weren't!

48
00:04:27,334 --> 00:04:30,498
Diana, I'm the last
person you can lie to.

49
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:33,106
I've known you
since you were born.

50
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:35,471
The first time I ever
laid eyes on you,

51
00:04:35,609 --> 00:04:40,513
you were laying there screaming your
head off, waving your fists in the air.

52
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:44,106
Your poor, beloved, sainted
mother... God rest her soul...

53
00:04:44,251 --> 00:04:47,221
Said that all babies do
that, but you never stopped.

54
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:48,845
Jack was used to it.

55
00:04:48,989 --> 00:04:51,356
We were married for
18 years, for God's sake.

56
00:04:51,491 --> 00:04:52,925
Well, maybe he got tired of it.

57
00:04:53,059 --> 00:04:56,894
So he leaves me for some
brain-dead finger painter?

58
00:04:57,030 --> 00:05:01,229
Diana, you've got
to face the facts.

59
00:05:01,368 --> 00:05:04,361
I know, I know, Ben. I'm trying.

60
00:05:04,504 --> 00:05:07,872
Maybe it was my temper,
maybe it wasn't. Oh, I don't know.

61
00:05:08,008 --> 00:05:09,772
All I know is I want him back!

62
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:12,311
- Did you tell him that?
- I don't know where he is!

63
00:05:12,445 --> 00:05:15,142
His attorneys do, but he told
them he'd fire them if they told me...

64
00:05:15,282 --> 00:05:16,807
And his accountants, too.

65
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,681
- How about his friends?
- Nobody has seen him.

66
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:23,484
Apparently, the only person
he socializes with now is Lisa.

67
00:05:23,623 --> 00:05:25,592
Oh, you've got to help me.

68
00:05:25,725 --> 00:05:27,353
Oh, no. No, Diana.

69
00:05:27,494 --> 00:05:30,191
Ben, please, tell him I'm
sorry. Tell him I'll change.

70
00:05:30,330 --> 00:05:33,323
Tell him things will be different.
Tell him he's got to talk to me again!

71
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:36,436
You say he met this
Lisa at an art gallery?

72
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:39,404
Yeah, the Westlake Art
Gallery on Ninth and Pine.

73
00:05:39,539 --> 00:05:41,235
She works there.

74
00:05:41,374 --> 00:05:43,843
Her full name is Lisa Sullivan.

75
00:05:43,977 --> 00:05:46,674
You go in there,
and you tell them...

76
00:05:46,813 --> 00:05:48,975
I... They won't talk to me.

77
00:05:49,115 --> 00:05:50,515
Diana...

78
00:05:51,885 --> 00:05:59,088
Uh, Diana, I don't want to get
involved in your personal affairs.

79
00:05:59,226 --> 00:06:00,285
Oh, Ben.

80
00:06:00,427 --> 00:06:01,895
Please, Ben.

81
00:06:02,028 --> 00:06:03,690
Please.

82
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:08,165
I know I'm not exactly your
favorite cousin, but my mother was.

83
00:06:08,301 --> 00:06:11,499
For her sake, Ben, please.

84
00:06:11,638 --> 00:06:13,129
Please, please, please.

85
00:06:13,273 --> 00:06:16,334
Oh, Diana.

86
00:06:16,476 --> 00:06:18,570
I can't. I...

87
00:06:19,813 --> 00:06:21,247
Well...

88
00:06:24,985 --> 00:06:26,544
I'll do what I can.

89
00:06:26,686 --> 00:06:28,052
Oh, thank you.

90
00:06:28,188 --> 00:06:29,198
Mm-mm-mm-mm-mm-mm.

91
00:06:29,222 --> 00:06:31,316
Thank you. Thank you.

92
00:06:31,458 --> 00:06:33,450
Oh, thank you, Ben dear.

93
00:06:33,593 --> 00:06:35,528
You're an absolute saint.

94
00:06:36,596 --> 00:06:38,155
But find him.

95
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:48,834
Thank you, Mrs. Nichols.

96
00:06:48,975 --> 00:06:51,410
- Enjoy.
- Thank you. We will.

97
00:06:51,544 --> 00:06:55,341
Boys. Joey... - - Joey!

98
00:06:55,482 --> 00:06:57,041
- Ha ha!
- Whew.

99
00:06:57,183 --> 00:06:59,209
Oh, my gosh, thank you.

100
00:06:59,352 --> 00:07:01,831
You're welcome. You should probably
get it out of the flight path, though.

101
00:07:01,855 --> 00:07:05,348
Yeah, well, I think they ought to forget
about dogs and start a leash law for kids.

102
00:07:05,492 --> 00:07:06,572
- Yeah.
- What do you think?

103
00:07:07,994 --> 00:07:09,929
Oh, can I help you
find something?

104
00:07:10,063 --> 00:07:11,691
Actually, I'm looking
for someone...

105
00:07:11,831 --> 00:07:13,094
- Oh.
- Lisa Sullivan.

106
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:14,599
Somebody said she works here.

107
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:16,498
Well, she used to work here.

108
00:07:16,636 --> 00:07:18,764
I took her place when she quit.

109
00:07:18,905 --> 00:07:23,001
Uh, do you have a
forwarding address for her?

110
00:07:23,143 --> 00:07:25,408
Why do you want
to find Lisa so bad?

111
00:07:25,545 --> 00:07:27,480
Actually, it's a long story.

112
00:07:27,614 --> 00:07:30,311
My wife, her name
is Lisa Sullivan, too,

113
00:07:30,450 --> 00:07:34,114
and earlier this year we got this
huge refund on our state income tax.

114
00:07:34,254 --> 00:07:35,916
The only thing is
we just moved here.

115
00:07:36,056 --> 00:07:38,082
We didn't pay any state
income tax last year,

116
00:07:38,224 --> 00:07:39,556
so we keep trying to tell them

117
00:07:39,693 --> 00:07:41,737
that they've got the wrong
Lisa Sullivan on their computer.

118
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:44,925
But every time we send them the check,
they keep sending it right back to us.

119
00:07:45,065 --> 00:07:46,776
Yeah, well, you know, I
haven't met a computer yet

120
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,598
- that can handle rejection.
- Exactly.

121
00:07:48,735 --> 00:07:50,465
So the other night
we were at this party,

122
00:07:50,603 --> 00:07:52,323
and we heard that Lisa
Sullivan worked here,

123
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:55,967
so here I am trying
to be a good guy.

124
00:07:56,109 --> 00:07:57,941
Well, what do you
know. An honest man.

125
00:07:58,078 --> 00:07:59,239
Come with me.

126
00:08:00,547 --> 00:08:03,608
Boy, I tell you, Lisa's
going to love you for this.

127
00:08:04,951 --> 00:08:09,855
Here it is: 1242
South Barrington.

128
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:11,151
Know where that is?

129
00:08:11,291 --> 00:08:13,556
- Yeah.
- Okay.

130
00:08:13,693 --> 00:08:15,924
30334.

131
00:08:16,062 --> 00:08:18,088
Oh, great. Thank you.
I really appreciate this.

132
00:08:18,231 --> 00:08:19,231
My pleasure.

133
00:08:19,332 --> 00:08:23,030
Uh, do you know
what she looks like?

134
00:08:23,169 --> 00:08:26,936
Well, yeah, you know, I think
I have a picture right here.

135
00:08:28,975 --> 00:08:29,999
Yeah.

136
00:08:32,278 --> 00:08:34,577
Now, this picture was taken
at the Lipscomb opening,

137
00:08:34,714 --> 00:08:37,240
which was about a year
ago, so it's pretty recent.

138
00:08:37,384 --> 00:08:39,046
And that is Lisa right there.

139
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:51,530
Yes?

140
00:08:51,598 --> 00:08:53,590
Hi, Lisa. I'm looking
for Jack Huntington.

141
00:08:53,733 --> 00:08:54,733
Wrong address.

142
00:08:54,868 --> 00:08:55,868
Wait. Wait.

143
00:08:55,935 --> 00:08:58,427
My name is Conrad McMasters.
I work for Ben Matlock.

144
00:08:58,571 --> 00:08:59,834
I just want to talk to him.

145
00:08:59,973 --> 00:09:00,973
Go away.

146
00:09:01,107 --> 00:09:02,769
It's okay, Lisa, let him in.

147
00:09:03,977 --> 00:09:05,070
Thank you.

148
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:06,702
Conrad?

149
00:09:06,846 --> 00:09:09,406
Jack, it's good
to see you again.

150
00:09:09,549 --> 00:09:11,575
Lisa Sullivan, Conrad McMasters.

151
00:09:11,718 --> 00:09:12,981
Now, what's this about?

152
00:09:13,119 --> 00:09:14,815
Well, Ben had me track you down.

153
00:09:14,954 --> 00:09:17,719
Apparently, your wife's
pretty anxious to talk to you.

154
00:09:17,857 --> 00:09:20,349
- Diana!
- Just walk away from me, huh, Jack?

155
00:09:20,493 --> 00:09:24,897
Thought I'd just dry up and blow
away like some old newspaper?

156
00:09:25,031 --> 00:09:26,727
For heaven's sake,
put that down, Diana!

157
00:09:26,866 --> 00:09:30,701
This time you really
made me mad, Jack!

158
00:09:30,837 --> 00:09:32,430
Let go of me. Let go of me!

159
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:34,183
I'm sorry about this,
Jack. I didn't mean it.

160
00:09:34,207 --> 00:09:35,402
You're nuts, Diana!

161
00:09:35,542 --> 00:09:37,568
Me? Oh, look at you.

162
00:09:37,710 --> 00:09:40,145
You're a bum... a
bum with a bimbo.

163
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:41,280
Get out!

164
00:09:41,347 --> 00:09:44,249
If you come back, so help
me I'll have you thrown in jail!

165
00:09:44,384 --> 00:09:45,716
I'd like to see that.

166
00:09:45,852 --> 00:09:49,380
I'd like to see you try that,
Mr... Mr... Mr. Young at Heart!

167
00:09:49,522 --> 00:09:51,162
- Come on! Diana, come on.
- God damn him!

168
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:52,850
- God damn him!
- Come on.

169
00:09:57,664 --> 00:10:01,192
Jack, your wife
needs serious help.

170
00:10:03,937 --> 00:10:06,270
You think I don't know that?

171
00:10:25,558 --> 00:10:26,558
Hello.

172
00:10:26,626 --> 00:10:30,757
Oh, Jack, I was just try...

173
00:10:31,764 --> 00:10:32,788
She what?

174
00:10:32,932 --> 00:10:34,059
Ben?

175
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:36,192
They just got here.

176
00:10:36,336 --> 00:10:38,828
Oh, I can't tell
you how sorry I am.

177
00:10:38,972 --> 00:10:39,837
Yes.

178
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:42,340
Yeah, sure. I'll be there.

179
00:10:42,475 --> 00:10:44,910
Okay, Diana, you used me.

180
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:47,104
Look, I was afraid that
even if you found him,

181
00:10:47,247 --> 00:10:49,716
- he still wouldn't talk to me.
- Well, who could blame him?

182
00:10:49,849 --> 00:10:51,477
You just went
after him with a gun.

183
00:10:51,618 --> 00:10:53,985
It wasn't loaded.

184
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:56,351
For God's sake, do
you think I'm crazy?

185
00:11:05,598 --> 00:11:10,229
♪ Get back into your room ♪

186
00:11:13,439 --> 00:11:17,900
♪ Let me see where you've been ♪

187
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:24,014
♪ I don't want to know ♪

188
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:29,646
♪ The story of your life ♪

189
00:11:29,789 --> 00:11:34,523
♪ Only tell me how you feel ♪

190
00:11:38,097 --> 00:11:41,693
♪ Would you like
to feel my lips ♪

191
00:11:41,834 --> 00:11:43,200
♪ Caress your shoulder? ♪

192
00:11:43,336 --> 00:11:45,737
Ben, how are you?
Good to see you.

193
00:11:45,872 --> 00:11:47,738
- Sit down.
- Yeah.

194
00:11:47,874 --> 00:11:49,069
Ahem.

195
00:11:50,543 --> 00:11:52,876
What kind of place is this?

196
00:11:53,012 --> 00:11:55,743
It's an old-fashioned, Greenwich
Village-type coffee house.

197
00:11:55,882 --> 00:11:57,578
A big hang-out for artists.

198
00:11:57,717 --> 00:11:59,413
Lisa turned me on to it.

199
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:02,079
What'll you have?

200
00:12:04,224 --> 00:12:05,692
Just bring one of these.

201
00:12:06,893 --> 00:12:08,259
Lisa wanted to come along,

202
00:12:08,394 --> 00:12:10,886
but she scored some kiln
time over at the college,

203
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:14,091
so she decided to spend
the afternoon throwing pots.

204
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:17,170
Oh, that's good. That's
good. Throwing pots is good.

205
00:12:18,238 --> 00:12:20,207
What's the deal with your hair?

206
00:12:22,108 --> 00:12:23,770
Letting it grow.

207
00:12:23,910 --> 00:12:25,173
Lisa's idea.

208
00:12:25,311 --> 00:12:28,213
Says I should flaunt
it while I've still got it.

209
00:12:30,116 --> 00:12:32,176
She has changed my life, Ben.

210
00:12:32,318 --> 00:12:34,514
Yeah, I see that. I...

211
00:12:34,654 --> 00:12:37,681
You know, she's
Beatrice to my Dante,

212
00:12:37,824 --> 00:12:40,760
my guide through a
universe I never knew existed.

213
00:12:40,893 --> 00:12:45,831
Before I met her, I thought art was
just something you invested money in.

214
00:12:45,965 --> 00:12:48,491
But I realize now it's an
investment of the soul.

215
00:12:48,635 --> 00:12:49,635
Here you go.

216
00:12:50,937 --> 00:12:53,930
That's double
mocha... Lisa's favorite.

217
00:12:59,012 --> 00:13:01,243
Boy, that's strong.

218
00:13:01,381 --> 00:13:03,179
Listen, I can't stay.

219
00:13:03,316 --> 00:13:08,516
I just stopped by to tell you how
sorry I am about what happened.

220
00:13:09,722 --> 00:13:12,123
Well, we both know
Diana's a little nuts.

221
00:13:12,258 --> 00:13:15,456
We had to put a double-lock
on the door and a bolt.

222
00:13:15,595 --> 00:13:18,861
Maybe she'll calm down
or move away or something.

223
00:13:18,998 --> 00:13:23,663
Conrad dropped this
picture up at Lisa's place.

224
00:13:23,803 --> 00:13:25,772
Do you happen to
know where he got it?

225
00:13:25,905 --> 00:13:29,433
Yeah, somebody at Westlake
Art Gallery gave it to him

226
00:13:29,575 --> 00:13:31,669
so he'd know what
Lisa looked like.

227
00:13:31,811 --> 00:13:36,613
Well, if you look close, you
can see that this guy here is me.

228
00:13:36,749 --> 00:13:39,913
But when this picture
was taken, I wasn't there.

229
00:13:40,053 --> 00:13:41,385
Say again?

230
00:13:41,521 --> 00:13:43,149
Okay.

231
00:13:43,289 --> 00:13:47,920
This guy here Lisa's with,
that's Howard Lipscomb.

232
00:13:48,061 --> 00:13:49,552
He's an artist.

233
00:13:49,696 --> 00:13:51,824
This picture was
taken last year,

234
00:13:51,964 --> 00:13:53,796
when his work was
on exhibit at the gallery.

235
00:13:53,933 --> 00:13:57,768
Howard Lipscomb attended the
VIP showing which opened the exhibit

236
00:13:57,904 --> 00:13:59,634
and then flew back to New York,

237
00:13:59,772 --> 00:14:01,968
and I came the night after,

238
00:14:02,108 --> 00:14:04,475
which was the grand
opening for the general public.

239
00:14:04,610 --> 00:14:08,047
And by then, Howard
Lipscomb was in New York.

240
00:14:08,181 --> 00:14:09,706
You see?

241
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:12,876
There's no way we could have
been in the same photograph.

242
00:14:13,953 --> 00:14:16,218
- You're saying this is a fake?
- It has to be.

243
00:14:16,356 --> 00:14:18,587
Part of this picture was
taken on the first night,

244
00:14:18,725 --> 00:14:20,235
and part of it was taken
on the second night.

245
00:14:20,259 --> 00:14:21,522
It's a composite.

246
00:14:22,695 --> 00:14:24,891
Well, why would
anybody want to do that?

247
00:14:25,031 --> 00:14:26,897
That's what I want
you to find out.

248
00:14:27,967 --> 00:14:29,902
- Me?
- You owe me, Ben.

249
00:14:30,036 --> 00:14:32,130
What if Diana had shot me?

250
00:14:32,271 --> 00:14:33,899
This is just a picture.

251
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:37,135
What if you had found out somebody
had faked a picture with you in it, huh?

252
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:40,644
You'd bust your butt trying to find
out what was going on, and you know it.

253
00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:41,804
Well...

254
00:14:41,948 --> 00:14:44,577
You could be going to
my funeral today, Ben.

255
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:46,515
Oh, come on, Jack.

256
00:14:46,652 --> 00:14:50,180
If it hadn't been for you, I never
would have even seen this damn picture.

257
00:15:17,283 --> 00:15:19,149
Sorry. Not interested.

258
00:15:19,285 --> 00:15:22,119
Because his work
stinks, that's why.

259
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:25,817
If you want to get him a
showing, try the animal shelter.

260
00:15:25,958 --> 00:15:28,792
Well, you have a nice day, too.

261
00:15:31,597 --> 00:15:34,032
Excuse me.

262
00:15:34,167 --> 00:15:37,160
Uh, are you in charge?

263
00:15:37,303 --> 00:15:40,034
Supposedly. I'm the
owner, Caroline Westlake.

264
00:15:40,173 --> 00:15:40,902
Ben Matlock.

265
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:43,407
- Ben Matlock? The lawyer?
- Oh, well...

266
00:15:43,543 --> 00:15:44,909
What a pleasure.

267
00:15:45,044 --> 00:15:46,672
And welcome to our new exhibit.

268
00:15:46,813 --> 00:15:49,112
- We're very excited about it.
- Oh.

269
00:15:50,283 --> 00:15:52,843
I've never seen
anything quite like this.

270
00:15:52,985 --> 00:15:56,285
Yes, these artists are
expressing the dramatic clash

271
00:15:56,422 --> 00:15:59,256
between freedom of form
and immutability of function.

272
00:15:59,392 --> 00:16:01,361
Oh, they are? Uh-huh.

273
00:16:02,995 --> 00:16:04,725
Oh, you like it?

274
00:16:04,864 --> 00:16:07,163
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

275
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,735
It's called "War and
Peace" by Clifford Alexander.

276
00:16:09,869 --> 00:16:11,132
Twenty-eight thousand dollars.

277
00:16:11,270 --> 00:16:13,398
You have excellent taste.

278
00:16:14,474 --> 00:16:15,908
Twenty-eight thousand?

279
00:16:17,009 --> 00:16:20,275
Well, that's a fair...

280
00:16:20,413 --> 00:16:23,611
Actually, I don't
know where I'd put it.

281
00:16:23,749 --> 00:16:25,342
Uh, could I...

282
00:16:25,485 --> 00:16:30,116
I came down here to ask
you about this photograph.

283
00:16:31,123 --> 00:16:33,991
I understand it was taken...

284
00:16:34,126 --> 00:16:37,392
Uh, I understand it was taken

285
00:16:37,530 --> 00:16:42,025
during a showing you had
for Howard Lipscomb last year.

286
00:16:42,168 --> 00:16:43,431
- That's right.
- Uh-huh.

287
00:16:43,569 --> 00:16:45,765
- Who took it? Do you remember?
- Gus Steinway.

288
00:16:45,905 --> 00:16:48,033
- He used to photograph all our events.
- Used to?

289
00:16:48,174 --> 00:16:50,166
He pulled up stakes and
moved to the Greek Isles.

290
00:16:50,309 --> 00:16:51,538
Midlife crisis.

291
00:16:53,613 --> 00:16:54,945
Is there a problem?

292
00:16:55,081 --> 00:16:57,016
- Do you know Jack Huntington?
- Very well.

293
00:16:57,149 --> 00:16:58,742
Well, he says that's a fake.

294
00:16:58,885 --> 00:17:00,353
A fake?

295
00:17:00,486 --> 00:17:02,887
Yeah. He says this side
of the picture was taken

296
00:17:03,022 --> 00:17:06,857
at the VIP showing when
Howard Lipscomb was here

297
00:17:06,993 --> 00:17:11,727
and this side was taken the next night
when Howard Lipscomb was not here.

298
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:18,669
I'm not expert, but this picture
sure looks like the real McCoy to me.

299
00:17:21,874 --> 00:17:23,365
Could I?

300
00:17:23,509 --> 00:17:25,239
- Oh. Of course.
- Thank you.

301
00:17:26,245 --> 00:17:27,907
Westlake Art Gallery.

302
00:17:31,417 --> 00:17:34,080
Uh, Lou, I'm going to
have to call you right back.

303
00:17:39,859 --> 00:17:41,088
It's me.

304
00:17:41,227 --> 00:17:43,526
We have a major problem.

305
00:17:43,663 --> 00:17:46,394
Somebody got that photograph.

306
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:49,198
<i>The</i> photograph.

307
00:17:50,670 --> 00:17:53,765
Well, someone already
has been asking questions.

308
00:17:53,906 --> 00:17:57,206
That lawyer, Ben Matlock.

309
00:17:57,343 --> 00:17:59,778
I don't care what
Caroline thinks.

310
00:17:59,912 --> 00:18:02,112
I know that picture's a
fake. I wasn't there that night.

311
00:18:02,181 --> 00:18:03,205
Tell him, sweetheart.

312
00:18:03,349 --> 00:18:05,250
He wasn't there,
Mr. Matlock. I remember.

313
00:18:05,384 --> 00:18:07,944
You see, to me, Howard
Lipscomb is a hero.

314
00:18:08,087 --> 00:18:12,149
Look, his work is bold and
brave and intellectually thrilling.

315
00:18:12,291 --> 00:18:15,921
I will never forget how excited
I was to finally get to meet him

316
00:18:16,062 --> 00:18:19,430
and how disappointed I was that
Jack couldn't share the experience.

317
00:18:19,565 --> 00:18:22,125
You sure you don't
want to have one?

318
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:25,363
No, the buzz I got from the
other one lasted till 4 this morning.

319
00:18:27,239 --> 00:18:28,502
So what's your next move?

320
00:18:28,641 --> 00:18:30,234
I don't know, Jack.

321
00:18:30,376 --> 00:18:33,210
This is just a picture, and
I've got a law firm to run.

322
00:18:33,346 --> 00:18:37,283
It's a fabrication, Ben, and
whoever fabricated it had a reason.

323
00:18:37,416 --> 00:18:39,579
Maybe I was matted
into that picture in order

324
00:18:39,603 --> 00:18:41,547
to cover up something
in the background.

325
00:18:41,687 --> 00:18:43,383
Well, you were there that night.

326
00:18:43,522 --> 00:18:47,516
Does anything look wrong or out of place
in this picture... I mean, besides Jack?

327
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:49,288
No.

328
00:18:49,428 --> 00:18:51,056
Who are these two fellows?

329
00:18:51,197 --> 00:18:52,507
Well, the big guy
is Dick Lerner.

330
00:18:52,531 --> 00:18:56,127
He owns some medical supply
company... Collects Pollocks.

331
00:18:56,268 --> 00:18:57,463
What about this guy?

332
00:18:57,603 --> 00:18:58,832
No. No, him I don't recognize.

333
00:19:02,541 --> 00:19:03,907
Hello, Ben.

334
00:19:06,579 --> 00:19:08,275
Lisa.

335
00:19:12,284 --> 00:19:13,582
Hello, Jack.

336
00:20:44,510 --> 00:20:45,876
- Hey!
- What?

337
00:21:33,425 --> 00:21:36,156
Uh, good morning.

338
00:21:36,295 --> 00:21:37,729
Good morning.

339
00:21:38,998 --> 00:21:40,557
Get the meaning?

340
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:46,836
Uh, crazy, mixed-up world.

341
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:49,066
No. It's called "Eggbeater."

342
00:21:49,208 --> 00:21:50,699
Oh.

343
00:21:50,843 --> 00:21:52,539
How can I help you?

344
00:21:52,678 --> 00:21:55,876
Do you remember that photograph
I brought in here yesterday?

345
00:21:56,015 --> 00:21:57,608
The one from the
Lipscomb exhibit?

346
00:21:57,750 --> 00:22:01,152
Yeah. It was stolen last night.

347
00:22:01,287 --> 00:22:02,482
You're kidding.

348
00:22:04,456 --> 00:22:08,791
Somebody broke in my house
and took it right off my desk.

349
00:22:08,928 --> 00:22:10,362
It's the only thing he stole.

350
00:22:10,496 --> 00:22:11,691
How strange.

351
00:22:11,831 --> 00:22:16,531
The thing is the only person outside
Jack Huntington and Lisa Sullivan

352
00:22:16,669 --> 00:22:18,729
who knew I had
the picture was you.

353
00:22:18,871 --> 00:22:20,931
Are you suggesting
that I stole it?

354
00:22:21,073 --> 00:22:24,771
No. I know I was knocked
over by a man last night.

355
00:22:24,910 --> 00:22:28,506
I just wonder if you told anybody
about the picture after I left.

356
00:22:28,647 --> 00:22:29,910
No.

357
00:22:30,049 --> 00:22:33,577
Wait, wait... I told
Randy about it.

358
00:22:33,719 --> 00:22:35,187
She gave it away,

359
00:22:35,321 --> 00:22:38,161
and I told her not to give anything
away again without checking with me.

360
00:22:41,026 --> 00:22:43,427
Ted, easy.

361
00:22:43,562 --> 00:22:45,895
I'm going to have to keep
an eye on these guys.

362
00:22:46,031 --> 00:22:47,795
Oh, yeah. Okay.

363
00:22:57,476 --> 00:23:00,207
I'll tell him. As a matter of
fact, he's sitting right here.

364
00:23:00,346 --> 00:23:01,939
I'll talk to you later.

365
00:23:02,081 --> 00:23:04,073
It was Caroline.

366
00:23:04,216 --> 00:23:06,481
Matlock's been in
asking more questions.

367
00:23:06,619 --> 00:23:07,896
She's mad, and
I don't blame her.

368
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:10,185
Well, he's shooting
blanks. Just relax.

369
00:23:10,322 --> 00:23:13,224
You had to go and
steal that photograph.

370
00:23:13,359 --> 00:23:16,921
This is a million dollar deal we've got
on the line here, don't you know that?

371
00:23:17,062 --> 00:23:18,997
Hey, my neck is on
the line here, fat boy.

372
00:23:19,131 --> 00:23:22,124
Look, maybe you can afford
to take chances, but I can't.

373
00:23:22,268 --> 00:23:24,169
I want you to shut up
and get off my back.

374
00:23:24,303 --> 00:23:26,465
What did you do with
the photograph, John?

375
00:23:26,605 --> 00:23:28,631
I burned it. End of problem.

376
00:23:28,774 --> 00:23:31,266
Yeah? Not necessarily.

377
00:23:59,405 --> 00:24:04,139
Hi. I'm looking for the person who
used to be Gus Steinway's partner.

378
00:24:04,276 --> 00:24:07,769
Yeah. That's me... Leon
Rousseau. What do you want?

379
00:24:07,913 --> 00:24:10,940
Well, I'm doing this book on Howard
Lipscomb... you know, the artist...

380
00:24:11,083 --> 00:24:15,350
And I understand that Gus
did some pictures of the exhibit

381
00:24:15,487 --> 00:24:16,598
that Howard had at
the Westlake Gallery.

382
00:24:16,622 --> 00:24:18,648
Could we get to the
bottom line? I'm kind of busy.

383
00:24:18,791 --> 00:24:20,331
Sure, well, if any
of the negatives are

384
00:24:20,355 --> 00:24:22,159
around, I'd like to take
a look through them.

385
00:24:22,294 --> 00:24:24,991
What, does this look
like a library to you?

386
00:24:25,130 --> 00:24:27,998
I'll pay you just
to look at them.

387
00:24:28,133 --> 00:24:30,193
And if I find something
I like, I'll pay you more.

388
00:24:30,336 --> 00:24:31,702
How does that sound?

389
00:24:31,837 --> 00:24:32,930
Wipe your feet.

390
00:24:43,115 --> 00:24:44,583
There.

391
00:24:49,355 --> 00:24:51,085
Knock yourself out.

392
00:24:51,223 --> 00:24:52,851
Is this stuff in
any kind of order?

393
00:24:52,992 --> 00:24:53,857
No.

394
00:24:53,993 --> 00:24:56,792
If you need me, I'll be across
the hall in the darkroom.

395
00:24:56,929 --> 00:24:59,797
Whatever you do,
knock first, okay?

396
00:24:59,932 --> 00:25:01,298
Gotcha.

397
00:25:01,433 --> 00:25:02,560
Ahh!

398
00:25:02,701 --> 00:25:07,264
That will be 50 bucks up front and
50 for each negative that you pull.

399
00:25:21,387 --> 00:25:22,480
Mr. Matlock, sir.

400
00:25:22,621 --> 00:25:25,284
Richard T. Lerner at
your service, Mr. Matlock...

401
00:25:25,424 --> 00:25:27,035
Unless, of course,
you've come to sue me.

402
00:25:27,059 --> 00:25:28,254
Oh, no, nothing like that.

403
00:25:28,394 --> 00:25:30,271
Can I get you something
to drink? Coffee? Juice?

404
00:25:30,295 --> 00:25:31,388
No, thank you.

405
00:25:31,530 --> 00:25:33,396
- Have a seat.
- Okay.

406
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:37,399
Well, you look fit as a fiddle,
so I know you're not a customer.

407
00:25:37,536 --> 00:25:38,970
No.

408
00:25:39,104 --> 00:25:42,506
What is it that you
sell, Mr. Lerner?

409
00:25:42,641 --> 00:25:44,007
Medical equipment.

410
00:25:44,143 --> 00:25:47,910
Oh. Well, you must
be doing pretty well.

411
00:25:48,047 --> 00:25:50,243
I see you collect art.

412
00:25:50,382 --> 00:25:52,146
Well, of sorts.

413
00:25:52,284 --> 00:25:55,152
I'm kind of partial to abstract
expressionism, as you see.

414
00:25:55,287 --> 00:25:56,287
Yeah.

415
00:25:56,422 --> 00:25:58,254
I hear that you
were at a showing

416
00:25:58,390 --> 00:26:01,656
that Westlake Galleries had
for Howard Lipscomb last year.

417
00:26:01,794 --> 00:26:04,423
Yes. I like Howard
Lipscomb's work.

418
00:26:04,563 --> 00:26:06,725
Actually, there
were two showings.

419
00:26:06,865 --> 00:26:11,997
There was one for the VIPs
and then one for the public.

420
00:26:12,137 --> 00:26:14,333
Which one did you go
to? Do you remember?

421
00:26:14,473 --> 00:26:17,204
Well, the VIP, naturally.

422
00:26:18,444 --> 00:26:21,380
But it was a year ago, as
you said. I could be mistaken.

423
00:26:21,513 --> 00:26:24,312
Do you remember whether you met
Howard Lipscomb when you were there?

424
00:26:24,450 --> 00:26:27,545
Oh, I've met Howard
half a dozen times.

425
00:26:27,686 --> 00:26:29,917
Maybe it was at the
opening, maybe not.

426
00:26:30,055 --> 00:26:34,789
Well... Again, these are
really, really very nice.

427
00:26:34,927 --> 00:26:36,828
Thank you for your time.

428
00:26:37,896 --> 00:26:38,896
Come in again.

429
00:26:39,031 --> 00:26:43,162
I always like it when famous people drop
in on me... not that they ever really do.

430
00:26:43,302 --> 00:26:44,302
Oh.

431
00:26:44,369 --> 00:26:46,861
Here, would you like a
malt ball for the road?

432
00:26:47,005 --> 00:26:48,268
Oh, no, I shouldn't.

433
00:26:48,407 --> 00:26:51,070
Neither should I, but I
mean, they're sitting right here.

434
00:26:51,210 --> 00:26:52,735
What the hell?

435
00:26:59,384 --> 00:27:00,477
Thank you.

436
00:27:18,237 --> 00:27:19,705
Hey, hey, hey.

437
00:27:25,978 --> 00:27:27,537
Aah!

438
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:36,889
Hey! Hey! Hey! Hey!

439
00:27:37,022 --> 00:27:38,217
Hey!

440
00:27:40,993 --> 00:27:43,792
Come on, man, that's
all my stuff. What the...

441
00:28:05,751 --> 00:28:08,220
Uh, coming.

442
00:28:08,353 --> 00:28:09,821
Coming.

443
00:28:11,523 --> 00:28:13,082
- Oh, Les.
- Ben.

444
00:28:13,225 --> 00:28:14,249
Want a sandwich?

445
00:28:14,393 --> 00:28:17,795
No time. Ben, I've got
some big news... big news.

446
00:28:17,930 --> 00:28:19,364
You'll never guess what it is.

447
00:28:19,498 --> 00:28:20,591
You won the lottery.

448
00:28:20,732 --> 00:28:23,201
No, no, this is big. Big-big.

449
00:28:23,335 --> 00:28:24,826
I mean really big.

450
00:28:24,970 --> 00:28:27,064
Um, you're going
around the world?

451
00:28:27,206 --> 00:28:27,935
No, no, no.

452
00:28:28,073 --> 00:28:33,034
This involves a person of the opposite
sex and a plan the two of us have.

453
00:28:33,178 --> 00:28:34,271
You're getting married.

454
00:28:34,413 --> 00:28:35,779
Who told you?

455
00:28:35,914 --> 00:28:40,113
Well, I think that's wonderful
news, Les. Congratulations.

456
00:28:40,252 --> 00:28:43,814
Sit down. What's the lucky lady's
name and how did you meet her?

457
00:28:43,956 --> 00:28:46,448
Her name is Mildred...
Mildred Novotny...

458
00:28:46,592 --> 00:28:49,494
And we met in the produce
section of the supermarket.

459
00:28:49,628 --> 00:28:53,395
She was trying to pick out a ripe
melon... You know, one of those Casabas?

460
00:28:53,532 --> 00:28:56,468
So she handed me one and
asked me if I thought it was ripe.

461
00:28:56,602 --> 00:28:59,595
So I gave it a squeeze and
told her I thought it was okay.

462
00:29:01,073 --> 00:29:04,601
Oh. So it all really
started over a melon, huh?

463
00:29:04,743 --> 00:29:07,008
Well, actually, it
started before that.

464
00:29:07,145 --> 00:29:10,047
You see, we were both
backing up our carts,

465
00:29:10,182 --> 00:29:12,014
you know, looking
at the produce,

466
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:16,281
and the next thing you know,
we backed into each other.

467
00:29:16,421 --> 00:29:17,980
- Backed right into each other?
- Yeah.

468
00:29:18,123 --> 00:29:24,586
And neither one of us moved for, I
don't know, it must have been 19 minutes.

469
00:29:24,730 --> 00:29:26,926
- Back to back?
- Back to back.

470
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:30,464
What is she, a widow?

471
00:29:30,602 --> 00:29:33,265
Yeah. Oh, and she
used to be a nurse.

472
00:29:35,641 --> 00:29:38,304
You know, when I was
young and used to date,

473
00:29:38,443 --> 00:29:41,003
I always felt safe with a nurse.

474
00:29:42,714 --> 00:29:44,492
Well, they know so much
about the body, you know.

475
00:29:44,516 --> 00:29:46,280
Yes. Yes. Yeah.

476
00:29:46,418 --> 00:29:49,820
Where are you going to
go on your honeymoon?

477
00:29:49,955 --> 00:29:53,357
Oh, Mildred wants to go
someplace she's never been before.

478
00:29:53,492 --> 00:29:58,328
Oh, someplace romantic
like Paris or Mexico?

479
00:29:58,463 --> 00:30:00,489
No, the Mayo Clinic.

480
00:30:02,467 --> 00:30:03,628
The Mayo Clinic?

481
00:30:03,769 --> 00:30:06,102
Well, yeah. I told you
she used to be a nurse.

482
00:30:06,238 --> 00:30:09,640
She wants to look around. You
know, watch a few operations.

483
00:30:10,776 --> 00:30:12,301
Gosh, I've got to go.

484
00:30:12,444 --> 00:30:14,003
I promised Mildred I'd meet her.

485
00:30:14,146 --> 00:30:16,809
- At the market?
- Yeah, to help her with her shopping.

486
00:30:18,283 --> 00:30:21,776
Les, you really
are a fast mover.

487
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:25,584
Well, Ben, time
waits for no man.

488
00:30:31,563 --> 00:30:32,963
Hey, Ben?

489
00:30:33,098 --> 00:30:34,464
In here.

490
00:30:36,668 --> 00:30:38,068
What happened to you face?

491
00:30:38,203 --> 00:30:40,695
Somebody jumped me
at the Steinway studio.

492
00:30:40,839 --> 00:30:42,137
They were after these.

493
00:30:43,308 --> 00:30:44,776
Proof sheets and negatives

494
00:30:44,910 --> 00:30:47,846
of the pictures Steinway shot at
the two Howard Lipscomb showings.

495
00:30:47,979 --> 00:30:49,208
Somebody jumped you?

496
00:30:49,348 --> 00:30:50,111
Yeah.

497
00:30:50,248 --> 00:30:52,149
Well, I got his license number.

498
00:30:52,284 --> 00:30:55,220
I get burglarized,
you get assaulted.

499
00:30:55,354 --> 00:30:57,448
Jack's right. Something
serious is going on here.

500
00:30:57,589 --> 00:30:58,613
No kidding.

501
00:31:00,058 --> 00:31:01,856
Lieutenant Brooks, please.

502
00:31:05,997 --> 00:31:08,364
I didn't expect you to
handle this personally, Bob.

503
00:31:08,500 --> 00:31:11,163
When I saw the list of priors on
this guy, I couldn't help myself.

504
00:31:11,303 --> 00:31:14,103
If he was wearing a ski mask, how
do you all know it's the same fellow?

505
00:31:14,239 --> 00:31:15,468
I'll know.

506
00:31:15,607 --> 00:31:20,875
This whole thing started
because Jack got a roving eye.

507
00:31:21,012 --> 00:31:23,948
If I was married to Diana,
I'd get a roving eye, too.

508
00:31:24,082 --> 00:31:26,642
My eye would fly
all over the place.

509
00:31:26,785 --> 00:31:29,220
Open up, Mr. Resnick.
It's the police.

510
00:31:34,826 --> 00:31:36,317
Mr. Resnick?

511
00:31:45,670 --> 00:31:48,105
That's the guy that
jumped me, Ben.

512
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:49,799
Same clothes, same build.

513
00:31:49,941 --> 00:31:52,035
- I know.
- How do you know?

514
00:31:53,412 --> 00:31:57,281
You know that third guy in the
picture that nobody recognized?

515
00:31:57,416 --> 00:31:59,146
That's him.

516
00:32:01,787 --> 00:32:03,153
There's one.

517
00:32:07,426 --> 00:32:09,190
Yeah, and there's the other.

518
00:32:09,327 --> 00:32:12,559
Those are the pictures that
were used to make the composite.

519
00:32:12,697 --> 00:32:16,429
You see? I wasn't at that
opening with Howard Lipscomb,

520
00:32:16,568 --> 00:32:17,661
and neither was Lerner.

521
00:32:17,803 --> 00:32:19,499
He lied to you, Ben.

522
00:32:19,638 --> 00:32:22,608
Whatever's going on here,
Lerner's in on it, I know it.

523
00:32:22,741 --> 00:32:24,300
I wish we had some proof.

524
00:32:24,443 --> 00:32:26,378
Proof? Somebody
steals the picture from you

525
00:32:26,511 --> 00:32:28,823
and then tries to steal the
negatives and then gets murdered.

526
00:32:28,847 --> 00:32:30,406
Now, what more
proof do you need?

527
00:32:30,549 --> 00:32:34,042
Well, something's going on, all
right, but dag-gone if I can figure it out.

528
00:32:34,186 --> 00:32:36,621
Somebody better
figure out something.

529
00:32:36,755 --> 00:32:38,917
I could wind up with
a bullet in my head.

530
00:32:51,369 --> 00:32:52,962
Oh, no.

531
00:32:53,104 --> 00:32:55,403
Jack? Jack.

532
00:32:55,540 --> 00:32:58,977
Jack, Jack, we have to talk.

533
00:32:59,110 --> 00:33:00,169
There's nothing to say.

534
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:01,871
Oh, now, Jack,
you don't mean that.

535
00:33:02,013 --> 00:33:04,346
- You don't get it. I want a new life.
- Go home, Diana.

536
00:33:04,483 --> 00:33:07,078
You can't abandon me
like some rusted old car!

537
00:33:07,219 --> 00:33:08,696
- Diana, go home.
- Stop this, will you, please?

538
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:10,916
- I hate you! I hate you! I hate you!
- Diana. Diana...

539
00:33:11,056 --> 00:33:13,855
Get her off my back!
I want her arrested!

540
00:33:13,992 --> 00:33:15,517
- Oh!
- Jack!

541
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:17,390
- Diana!
- Get her off my back!

542
00:33:17,529 --> 00:33:19,430
Get her out of here!

543
00:33:34,980 --> 00:33:36,642
Oh, Les.

544
00:33:36,781 --> 00:33:37,781
Hi, Ben.

545
00:33:37,883 --> 00:33:40,478
Sorry to barge in on you
like this, but I need your help.

546
00:33:40,619 --> 00:33:41,643
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

547
00:33:41,786 --> 00:33:43,618
Mildred wants a church wedding,

548
00:33:43,755 --> 00:33:47,385
and, well, we're trying to decide
what kind of music to have.

549
00:33:47,526 --> 00:33:49,995
Now, I suggested an
organ playing a medley...

550
00:33:50,128 --> 00:33:53,428
You know, "O Promise
Me," "I Love You Truly,"

551
00:33:53,565 --> 00:33:56,330
"There'll Be a Hot Time
in the Old Town Tonight."

552
00:33:56,468 --> 00:33:59,336
I'd go for "Hot Time in
the Old Town Tonight."

553
00:34:00,972 --> 00:34:05,967
Yeah, but Mildred wants something
different, something more personal.

554
00:34:06,111 --> 00:34:08,273
Oh, personal.

555
00:34:08,413 --> 00:34:11,008
Boy, you're... you're really...

556
00:34:11,149 --> 00:34:13,175
You're really stuck
on Mildred, aren't you?

557
00:34:13,318 --> 00:34:14,513
Well...

558
00:34:16,721 --> 00:34:19,247
So something personal.

559
00:34:19,391 --> 00:34:20,415
Personal.

560
00:34:20,559 --> 00:34:23,358
Personal. Um, um...

561
00:34:23,495 --> 00:34:27,830
Why don't we sing her a
duet and dedicate it to her?

562
00:34:27,966 --> 00:34:30,936
- You think so?
- Oh, yeah.

563
00:34:31,069 --> 00:34:32,469
What could we sing?

564
00:34:32,604 --> 00:34:35,802
Didn't you say she spent
time in South Carolina?

565
00:34:35,941 --> 00:34:38,103
- Yeah, she loved it there.
- Yeah?

566
00:34:38,243 --> 00:34:40,439
Well, how about "Carolina Moon?"

567
00:34:40,579 --> 00:34:42,878
Think she'll like it?

568
00:34:43,014 --> 00:34:47,110
- Oh, she'd love it. I bet she'd cry.
- Yeah.

569
00:34:48,186 --> 00:34:50,121
Of course, I don't know.

570
00:34:50,255 --> 00:34:55,091
I don't know. You're so
sensitive, you might cry yourself.

571
00:34:55,226 --> 00:34:57,092
Oh, come on.

572
00:34:57,228 --> 00:34:59,925
Okay. Okay, let's
give it a try, want to?

573
00:35:00,065 --> 00:35:01,089
Okay.

574
00:35:01,232 --> 00:35:04,361
♪

575
00:35:04,502 --> 00:35:08,496
♪ Carolina moon ♪

576
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:12,543
♪ Keeps shining ♪

577
00:35:12,677 --> 00:35:18,844
♪ Shining on the one
who waits for me ♪

578
00:35:18,984 --> 00:35:21,579
♪ Oh, Mildred ♪

579
00:35:21,720 --> 00:35:28,650
♪ Carolina moon, I'm pining ♪

580
00:35:28,793 --> 00:35:31,854
♪ Pining for the... ♪

581
00:35:34,065 --> 00:35:35,397
Are you all right?

582
00:35:35,533 --> 00:35:38,264
I'm fine. I'm fine.

583
00:35:38,403 --> 00:35:39,427
Are you crying?

584
00:35:39,571 --> 00:35:41,062
No, no, it's my sinuses.

585
00:35:41,206 --> 00:35:44,370
I've got to go home
and use my nasal spray.

586
00:35:46,745 --> 00:35:49,214
♪♪

587
00:36:00,592 --> 00:36:01,685
You know?

588
00:36:03,395 --> 00:36:06,456
I'll bet you anything that
photographer dummied up the picture

589
00:36:06,598 --> 00:36:10,330
and then left for Greece because
John Resnick paid him to go.

590
00:36:15,206 --> 00:36:16,765
But where did he get the money?

591
00:36:21,646 --> 00:36:24,047
You think he was
blackmailing someone?

592
00:36:30,555 --> 00:36:32,717
This is making me crazy.

593
00:36:40,165 --> 00:36:42,600
You make me crazy, too.

594
00:36:42,734 --> 00:36:44,635
I'm trying to work.

595
00:36:46,237 --> 00:36:47,899
I like it.

596
00:36:48,039 --> 00:36:50,338
- It's terrible.
- Mm-mm.

597
00:36:51,576 --> 00:36:53,272
Oh, Jack.

598
00:36:53,411 --> 00:36:56,848
You know Randy McGowan, that
girl that took my place at the gallery?

599
00:36:56,981 --> 00:36:58,916
She called me today,

600
00:36:59,050 --> 00:37:01,110
and apparently all this
fake photograph stuff

601
00:37:01,252 --> 00:37:03,744
has gotten Caroline
real ticked off.

602
00:37:03,888 --> 00:37:09,418
You know how Caroline is.
She's been giving me a bad rap.

603
00:37:09,561 --> 00:37:12,121
- To whom?
- To everybody.

604
00:37:12,263 --> 00:37:14,858
Why is she badmouthing you?

605
00:37:14,999 --> 00:37:19,528
Because I'm what started it
all... At least in her mind I am.

606
00:37:22,774 --> 00:37:26,677
Jack, art is my whole life.

607
00:37:27,779 --> 00:37:29,748
People listen to Caroline.

608
00:37:31,049 --> 00:37:33,951
She's going to ruin my
career before I even have one.

609
00:37:36,187 --> 00:37:37,746
No, she's not.

610
00:37:38,823 --> 00:37:40,086
I promise.

611
00:37:41,226 --> 00:37:42,387
Keep painting.

612
00:37:42,527 --> 00:37:44,587
I'll pick up dinner
on my way home.

613
00:37:44,729 --> 00:37:47,631
And I hope to hell I
don't run into Diana.

614
00:37:47,766 --> 00:37:49,997
- Home from where?
- The gallery.

615
00:37:50,135 --> 00:37:51,831
I'm going to
apologize to Caroline.

616
00:37:51,970 --> 00:37:55,532
I'll tell her from now on if anybody
asks her about this damn photograph,

617
00:37:55,673 --> 00:37:57,505
I'll personally punch
them in the nose.

618
00:38:00,645 --> 00:38:02,307
How good are you at seascapes?

619
00:38:02,447 --> 00:38:05,281
I don't know. I never tried it.

620
00:38:05,416 --> 00:38:07,112
Then pack your paints.

621
00:38:07,252 --> 00:38:09,744
Tomorrow we'll leave
for the Caribbean.

622
00:38:26,271 --> 00:38:27,271
Hi, Randy.

623
00:38:27,405 --> 00:38:28,845
Hi, Jack. I'm sorry.
We just closed.

624
00:38:28,907 --> 00:38:30,102
Is Caroline still here?

625
00:38:30,241 --> 00:38:32,210
Yeah, but she's meeting
some people in her office.

626
00:38:32,343 --> 00:38:33,943
I'll just stick my
head in for a minute.

627
00:38:33,978 --> 00:38:35,378
Can't this wait until tomorrow?

628
00:38:35,513 --> 00:38:38,847
Well, we're leaving town, and I've
got to clear the air about Lisa, okay?

629
00:38:48,359 --> 00:38:50,260
- Jack.
- Hi, Caroline.

630
00:38:50,395 --> 00:38:52,159
This will just take a minute.

631
00:38:53,331 --> 00:38:55,926
You like Andy Warhol?

632
00:38:56,067 --> 00:38:57,067
Not particularly.

633
00:38:57,135 --> 00:38:59,627
Look, it's my fault

634
00:38:59,771 --> 00:39:02,832
Ben Matlock's been hassling you
about this photograph, not Lisa's.

635
00:39:02,974 --> 00:39:05,569
I was curious about it, and
he was doing me a favor.

636
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:08,805
So if you're upset, I'm sorry.

637
00:39:08,947 --> 00:39:10,540
I'm dropping the whole matter.

638
00:39:10,682 --> 00:39:14,414
And if you're carrying a
grudge, I'm your target, not Lisa.

639
00:39:15,653 --> 00:39:16,882
Okay?

640
00:39:17,021 --> 00:39:19,354
If you say it's over, it's over.

641
00:39:19,490 --> 00:39:22,858
I don't hold grudges.
I don't have time.

642
00:39:22,994 --> 00:39:25,122
Thanks, Caroline.

643
00:39:26,497 --> 00:39:27,988
Sorry to interrupt.

644
00:39:28,132 --> 00:39:29,225
Bye.

645
00:39:35,740 --> 00:39:37,641
I can't stand his work.

646
00:39:37,775 --> 00:39:38,775
Whose?

647
00:39:38,910 --> 00:39:40,401
Warhol's.

648
00:39:40,545 --> 00:39:42,013
Well, this isn't a Warhol.

649
00:39:42,146 --> 00:39:43,876
The one they're
looking at in there.

650
00:39:44,015 --> 00:39:45,677
Good night, Randy.

651
00:39:45,817 --> 00:39:47,410
Good night, Jack.

652
00:40:00,999 --> 00:40:02,126
Ben.

653
00:40:03,234 --> 00:40:05,601
I just uncovered some
information about John Resnick

654
00:40:05,737 --> 00:40:07,057
I thought you'd
be interested in.

655
00:40:07,105 --> 00:40:08,903
Are you in the mood to hear it?

656
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:11,635
No, but tell me anyway.

657
00:40:18,116 --> 00:40:21,450
I'm so jittery, I
can't stand still.

658
00:40:21,586 --> 00:40:24,317
Time for my run anyway.
Maybe that will help.

659
00:40:26,524 --> 00:40:29,517
- I'll be back in 40 minutes.
- Oh, I don't know what to pack.

660
00:40:29,661 --> 00:40:31,129
Hmm.

661
00:40:37,235 --> 00:40:38,567
You're packed.

662
00:40:44,976 --> 00:40:48,174
His name was Hugo
Wyckoff, a local artist,

663
00:40:48,313 --> 00:40:51,750
a lithographer who died about
a year ago in a hit-and-run.

664
00:40:51,883 --> 00:40:54,045
What's he got to do
with John Resnick?

665
00:40:54,185 --> 00:40:56,711
Well, when the police
questioned Wyckoff's widow,

666
00:40:56,854 --> 00:41:00,256
she said that he told her
he was afraid for his life.

667
00:41:00,391 --> 00:41:04,795
Now, he didn't say why he was afraid,
but he did say of whom... John Resnick.

668
00:41:04,929 --> 00:41:07,256
But as it turns out,
John Resnick had an

669
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:09,867
alibi for the night Hugo
Wyckoff was killed.

670
00:41:10,001 --> 00:41:13,062
He was at the opening of an art
exhibit at the Westlake Gallery.

671
00:41:13,204 --> 00:41:16,504
The Howard Lipscomb Exhibit?

672
00:41:16,641 --> 00:41:19,406
Uh-huh. And when the
police checked out the gallery,

673
00:41:19,544 --> 00:41:21,069
they were shown a photograph

674
00:41:21,212 --> 00:41:24,740
that proved John Resnick was
there at the time of the hit-and-run,

675
00:41:24,882 --> 00:41:27,647
so Resnick was
dropped as a suspect.

676
00:41:27,785 --> 00:41:32,086
They dummied up that photograph
to give John Resnick an alibi?

677
00:41:32,223 --> 00:41:33,623
Probably.

678
00:41:33,758 --> 00:41:35,351
Oh, one more thing.

679
00:41:35,493 --> 00:41:41,262
John Resnick's two best friends were
Caroline Westlake and Dick Lerner.

680
00:41:41,399 --> 00:41:45,837
On the night before he died, he made
a series of phone calls after 1 a.m.:

681
00:41:45,970 --> 00:41:50,965
two to Miss Westlake, one to Dick Lerner,
and two to somebody named Carl Shepley.

682
00:41:51,109 --> 00:41:55,069
So all of them are in on this
photograph fraud business.

683
00:41:55,213 --> 00:41:57,739
They hired John
Resnick to kill that artist

684
00:41:57,882 --> 00:42:00,750
and then killed John Resnick
because he knew too much.

685
00:42:00,885 --> 00:42:03,445
But knew too much about what?

686
00:42:03,588 --> 00:42:06,490
I don't know, but I'll tell
you what I'm not going to do.

687
00:42:06,624 --> 00:42:07,421
What's that?

688
00:42:07,558 --> 00:42:08,992
Tell Jack Huntington.

689
00:42:09,127 --> 00:42:10,993
It would put him
right over the edge.

690
00:42:31,015 --> 00:42:33,382
- Short cut.
- Okay.

691
00:43:17,862 --> 00:43:21,355
The license plate
you saw was ESV...

692
00:43:27,905 --> 00:43:29,339
Excuse me, Miss Sullivan.

693
00:43:29,474 --> 00:43:31,841
I'm very sorry, and I
know this is painful,

694
00:43:31,976 --> 00:43:33,740
but if you could
answer some questions,

695
00:43:33,878 --> 00:43:36,006
it might help us to find
the person who did this.

696
00:43:36,147 --> 00:43:37,206
I'll try.

697
00:43:37,348 --> 00:43:39,977
According to the skid
marks and an eyewitness,

698
00:43:40,118 --> 00:43:41,586
this wasn't just an accident.

699
00:43:41,719 --> 00:43:43,813
Now, somebody meant to kill him.

700
00:43:43,955 --> 00:43:45,947
Did he always run at this time?

701
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:47,285
Yes.

702
00:43:47,425 --> 00:43:50,293
Well, last night, did
he make any calls

703
00:43:50,428 --> 00:43:52,624
or maybe have an argument
with somebody over the phone?

704
00:43:52,763 --> 00:43:54,197
No.

705
00:43:54,332 --> 00:43:56,699
He did go out for about an hour.

706
00:43:56,834 --> 00:43:57,893
Do you know where he went?

707
00:43:58,035 --> 00:44:02,268
To the art gallery to
talk to Caroline Westlake.

708
00:44:02,406 --> 00:44:04,551
- Lieutenant?
- Excuse me for just a minute.

709
00:44:04,575 --> 00:44:05,838
We have a make on that car.

710
00:44:05,977 --> 00:44:07,688
If only he hadn't gone
running this morning.

711
00:44:07,712 --> 00:44:10,876
- I know, I know.
- ESV 571...

712
00:44:11,015 --> 00:44:14,679
Why did Jack want to
talk to Caroline Westlake?

713
00:44:14,819 --> 00:44:19,154
So he could apologize for all the
trouble the picture of him had caused.

714
00:44:20,892 --> 00:44:22,383
Say, I'd better get going.

715
00:44:22,527 --> 00:44:24,393
Um, I'll be in
touch, Miss Sullivan.

716
00:44:24,529 --> 00:44:25,792
Have you got something?

717
00:44:25,930 --> 00:44:30,300
Yeah, the eyewitness says that the car
that hit Jack was a blue Lincoln Mark VII.

718
00:44:31,402 --> 00:44:32,461
Jack's dead?

719
00:44:32,603 --> 00:44:34,663
- Yeah.
- I can't believe it.

720
00:44:34,805 --> 00:44:37,673
- Jack's dead.
- Here.

721
00:44:37,808 --> 00:44:40,277
What did you want to see my car?

722
00:44:40,411 --> 00:44:42,642
When did you last drive it?

723
00:44:42,780 --> 00:44:44,214
Yesterday.

724
00:44:44,348 --> 00:44:46,374
You didn't drive
it this morning?

725
00:44:46,517 --> 00:44:48,611
I just told you no.

726
00:44:55,726 --> 00:44:57,888
Oh, my God.

727
00:44:58,029 --> 00:44:59,622
Did it look like this yesterday?

728
00:44:59,764 --> 00:45:02,199
What happened to it?

729
00:45:02,333 --> 00:45:05,064
I'm afraid I'm going to have
to place you under arrest.

730
00:45:09,874 --> 00:45:13,003
I can't believe it, my
beloved Jack dead.

731
00:45:13,144 --> 00:45:14,772
I'm shocked. Shocked!

732
00:45:14,912 --> 00:45:16,676
How can anyone
think I'd kill him?

733
00:45:16,814 --> 00:45:20,581
Maybe because you assaulted
him twice... Once with a gun.

734
00:45:20,718 --> 00:45:22,277
But I told you the
gun wasn't loaded.

735
00:45:22,420 --> 00:45:25,185
- Once in front of a police lieutenant.
- I wasn't trying to kill him.

736
00:45:25,323 --> 00:45:29,317
Diana, when people see a woman
waving a gun around, making threats,

737
00:45:29,460 --> 00:45:31,326
they tend to take
that seriously.

738
00:45:31,462 --> 00:45:33,556
Unless she said, "Just kidding,"

739
00:45:33,698 --> 00:45:36,668
or she fires it and a little sign
comes out and says "Bang,"

740
00:45:36,801 --> 00:45:39,134
people tend to believe
what they see and hear.

741
00:45:39,270 --> 00:45:40,704
But anyone who knows me...

742
00:45:40,838 --> 00:45:44,366
Knows that your life has
been one long temper tantrum.

743
00:45:44,508 --> 00:45:46,602
- Where the hell do you think you're going?
- Home.

744
00:45:46,744 --> 00:45:48,372
- Are you going to defend me?
- No.

745
00:45:48,512 --> 00:45:50,071
You've got to defend me.

746
00:45:50,214 --> 00:45:52,274
Give me one good reason why.

747
00:45:52,416 --> 00:45:53,416
I'm innocent.

748
00:45:53,451 --> 00:45:56,478
Then any lawyer can handle
it, and you don't need me.

749
00:45:56,621 --> 00:45:58,385
- Ben.
- What?

750
00:45:58,522 --> 00:46:02,721
If my mother was asking,
would that be your answer?

751
00:46:02,860 --> 00:46:06,888
Ben, at the end, I took good
care of her, and you know it.

752
00:46:11,669 --> 00:46:13,035
You're going to
defend this woman?

753
00:46:13,170 --> 00:46:14,661
Yeah.

754
00:46:14,805 --> 00:46:16,398
She's a nut case.

755
00:46:16,540 --> 00:46:18,236
I know it, but she's family.

756
00:46:18,376 --> 00:46:19,571
Well, how about the gun?

757
00:46:19,710 --> 00:46:21,576
It wasn't loaded.

758
00:46:21,712 --> 00:46:24,272
What about the little
incident outside the house?

759
00:46:24,415 --> 00:46:26,646
Well, she just beat
him up with her purse.

760
00:46:26,784 --> 00:46:28,912
You must be as nutty as she is.

761
00:46:29,053 --> 00:46:31,545
Diana is not the kind
of woman that kills men.

762
00:46:31,689 --> 00:46:33,487
She just makes them
wish they were dead.

763
00:46:33,624 --> 00:46:37,026
Right. So you're going to defend her
so she can do the same thing to you.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
